  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Commerce has obligated $3.5B in contracts in FY2026 to date. The agency executed 6,891 awards with an average award size of $513K. Obligations fell 14.9% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations rose and fell across the last six years: FY21 $4.5B, FY22 $4.1B, FY23 $5B, FY24 $5.2B, FY25 $4.2B, FY26 $3.5B. The largest spending categories in FY2026 to date are Other Computer Related Services ($651M) and Computer Systems Design Services ($538M). The Top 10 vendor list is led by REED TECHNOLOGY AND INFORMATION SERVICES LLC at $218M, followed by ACCENTURE FEDERAL SERVICES LLC at $111M and LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION at $108M. This mix shows heavy investment in IT and professional services alongside targeted spending in ship building and aircraft manufacturing. These figures are contract obligations and the FY2026 total is a fiscal year to date number.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Commerce contract spending changed recently?",
        "body": "The Department of Commerce obligated $3.5B in FY2026 to date, down 14.9% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations were $4.5B in FY21, $4.1B in FY22, $5B in FY23, $5.2B in FY24, and $4.2B in FY25. The FY2026 total is a fiscal year to date number."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which categories and vendors dominate Commerce contracting?",
        "body": "Other Computer Related Services leads categories at $651M, followed by Computer Systems Design Services at $538M and Custom Computer Programming Services at $367M. REED TECHNOLOGY AND INFORMATION SERVICES LLC is the largest vendor at $218M, with ACCENTURE FEDERAL SERVICES LLC at $111M and LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION at $108M. The Top 10 categories and Top 10 vendors reflect a concentration in IT and professional services plus pockets of ship building and aircraft manufacturing."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Department of Commerce spend on contracts?",
      "a": "The Department of Commerce has obligated $3.5B in contracts in FY2026 to date. The agency executed 6,891 awards with an average award size of $513K, and obligations were down 14.9% year over year."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Commerce buy?",
      "a": "Commerce buys IT and professional services, along with ship building, aircraft work, and R&D. The Top 10 categories in FY2026 to date include Other Computer Related Services at $651M, Computer Systems Design Services at $538M, Custom Computer Programming Services at $367M, All Other Publishers at $220M, Administrative Management and General Management Consulting Services at $154M, Ship Building and Repairing at $150M, All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services at $137M, Surveying and Mapping (except Geophysical) Services at $127M, Aircraft Manufacturing at $108M, and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) at $93.3M."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who are Commerce's largest contractors?",
      "a": "The largest contractors are REED TECHNOLOGY AND INFORMATION SERVICES LLC at $218M, ACCENTURE FEDERAL SERVICES LLC at $111M, and LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION at $108M. Other top vendors include JAG KETCHIKAN, LLC at $99.6M; EARTH RESOURCES TECHNOLOGY, LLC at $99M; BOOZ ALLEN HAMILTON INC at $95.9M; PERATON INC. at $86.1M; IGNITEACTION LLC at $81.8M; SCIENCE APPLICATIONS INTERNATIONAL CORPORATION at $70.1M; and FOUR POINTS TECHNOLOGY, L.L.C. at $58.6M."
